1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,310
Imagine that tomorrow,
something unexpected happens.

2
00:00:04,220 --> 00:00:08,910
One country invades another. And we say:
"Enough. This is against the rules. Stop."

3
00:00:10,891 --> 00:00:12,981
But... who gets to order them to stop?

4
00:00:13,531 --> 00:00:15,141
Who is actually above the countries?

5
00:00:16,195 --> 00:00:19,065
The answer is surprisingly simple. Nobody.

6
00:00:21,788 --> 00:00:23,518
There is the United Nations.

7
00:00:24,448 --> 00:00:28,818
There are international courts, treaties,
and countless international organisations.

8
00:00:30,014 --> 00:00:32,554
But there is no world government
with its own police and

9
00:00:32,634 --> 00:00:36,824
military that can simply say:
"This country has broken the rules.

10
00:00:37,054 --> 00:00:39,364
And now we are going to stop it."

11
00:00:40,075 --> 00:00:44,375
And that is why international
politics is, in a sense, anarchic.

12
00:00:44,915 --> 00:00:47,585
Not because the world
is necessarily chaotic.

13
00:00:48,535 --> 00:00:50,655
But because there is no
higher authority above

14
00:00:50,735 --> 00:00:54,265
sovereign states, with a
monopoly on the use of force.

15
00:00:55,074 --> 00:00:58,184
And that may be one of the biggest
problems in the world today.

16
00:01:01,114 --> 00:01:02,964
Imagine an ordinary person.

17
00:01:03,394 --> 00:01:07,344
They can't simply say: "I don't like
this law, so I won't follow it."

18
00:01:08,017 --> 00:01:10,567
If they break the law,
there is a police force.

19
00:01:11,157 --> 00:01:13,447
If they have a dispute, there is a court.

20
00:01:14,302 --> 00:01:18,612
And if someone attacks another person,
the state can use force to stop them.

21
00:01:19,326 --> 00:01:23,376
Why? Because the state has a monopoly
on the legitimate use of force.

22
00:01:24,158 --> 00:01:26,128
We don't have to agree with every law.

23
00:01:26,258 --> 00:01:28,598
But we all know that there is someone who

24
00:01:28,678 --> 00:01:31,448
ultimately has the
authority to enforce it.

25
00:01:32,107 --> 00:01:34,027
And that is what makes
it possible for millions

26
00:01:34,107 --> 00:01:36,697
of people to live
together under one system.

27
00:01:39,617 --> 00:01:42,807
Now let's ask the same question
at the international level.

28
00:01:43,597 --> 00:01:46,567
Imagine a small country
attacks its neighbour.

29
00:01:46,917 --> 00:01:50,167
Who comes and arrests its
prime minister? Nobody.

30
00:01:51,109 --> 00:01:55,399
China invades another country.
Who sends the police? Nobody.

31
00:01:56,263 --> 00:01:59,273
The United States violates
an international treaty.

32
00:02:00,123 --> 00:02:04,373
Who can order the United States:
"Stop. Fix this immediately."

33
00:02:05,323 --> 00:02:09,533
Again, there is no world police force
with authority over the United States.

34
00:02:10,563 --> 00:02:13,073
Of course, there are
international institutions.

35
00:02:14,146 --> 00:02:18,496
There are courts. There is the
United Nations. There are sanctions.

36
00:02:19,326 --> 00:02:21,716
There are diplomatic
and military coalitions.

37
00:02:22,587 --> 00:02:26,357
But ultimately, these mechanisms
depend on the countries themselves.

38
00:02:27,247 --> 00:02:29,077
And that creates a strange situation.

39
00:02:30,152 --> 00:02:32,212
Countries create the rules, but countries

40
00:02:32,292 --> 00:02:34,962
are also the main
enforcers of those rules.

41
00:02:37,852 --> 00:02:39,482
At first, it sounds simple.

42
00:02:40,232 --> 00:02:41,902
Let's create a world government.

43
00:02:42,572 --> 00:02:44,642
Every country gives it some of its powers.

44
00:02:45,675 --> 00:02:47,265
We create a world parliament.

45
00:02:47,735 --> 00:02:49,085
A world court.

46
00:02:49,575 --> 00:02:51,885
A world police force.
And a world military.

47
00:02:52,793 --> 00:02:57,613
When one country causes trouble, the world
government intervenes. Problem solved.

48
00:02:57,693 --> 00:02:58,893
Except...

49
00:03:00,061 --> 00:03:02,051
which country would agree to that?

50
00:03:02,861 --> 00:03:04,361
Imagine the world
government tells the United

51
00:03:04,441 --> 00:03:07,871
States: "Your military
cannot cross this border."

52
00:03:08,735 --> 00:03:10,945
And the United States says: "No."

53
00:03:13,215 --> 00:03:14,525
What happens next?

54
00:03:15,355 --> 00:03:19,485
If the world government has no force of
its own, the order is just a suggestion.

55
00:03:20,396 --> 00:03:23,376
If it does have force, then
it has to be capable of using

56
00:03:23,456 --> 00:03:27,186
that force even against the most
powerful countries on Earth.

57
00:03:27,959 --> 00:03:29,749
And that is the real problem.

58
00:03:30,539 --> 00:03:34,069
For a world government to work, it would
actually have to be above the countries.

59
00:03:37,149 --> 00:03:40,159
And here we get to an
interesting historical detour.

60
00:03:41,009 --> 00:03:44,009
After the collapse of the
Soviet Union, the world entered

61
00:03:44,089 --> 00:03:46,919
a period when there was
one clearly dominant power.

62
00:03:47,785 --> 00:03:50,765
The United States. It
had an enormous economy.

63
00:03:50,845 --> 00:03:52,555
An enormous military.

64
00:03:53,065 --> 00:03:56,255
Military bases around the
world. Powerful allies.

65
00:03:57,208 --> 00:04:00,798
And the ability to project force
almost anywhere on the planet.

66
00:04:01,728 --> 00:04:05,598
Some people therefore started talking
about an American unipolar world.

67
00:04:06,351 --> 00:04:08,421
But there is an important difference.

68
00:04:09,191 --> 00:04:12,861
The United States was not a world
government. It was a hegemon.

69
00:04:14,046 --> 00:04:17,476
It had an extraordinary ability
to influence other countries.

70
00:04:17,666 --> 00:04:21,576
But it did not have universal
legal authority over them.

71
00:04:22,229 --> 00:04:26,419
And more importantly: Who had the
authority to control the United States?

72
00:04:26,769 --> 00:04:28,499
Nobody above it.

73
00:04:29,175 --> 00:04:32,365
And that reveals a fundamental
problem with every hegemon.

74
00:04:33,075 --> 00:04:36,225
If we create one power strong
enough to stop everyone else...

75
00:04:36,795 --> 00:04:38,325
who stops that power?

76
00:04:40,955 --> 00:04:43,565
Let's imagine that we
really wanted to build one.

77
00:04:44,315 --> 00:04:47,085
We could create something
like a Federation of Earth.

78
00:04:47,901 --> 00:04:49,451
Countries would still exist.

79
00:04:50,321 --> 00:04:51,751
Brazil would still be Brazil.

80
00:04:52,481 --> 00:04:54,131
France would still be France.

81
00:04:54,781 --> 00:04:56,311
Japan would still be Japan.

82
00:04:57,219 --> 00:04:59,989
But some powers would be
transferred to a higher level.

83
00:05:00,899 --> 00:05:05,009
For example: wars between countries,
weapons of mass destruction,

84
00:05:05,379 --> 00:05:08,019
international aggression,
certain global environmental

85
00:05:08,099 --> 00:05:11,489
problems, and perhaps the
protection of global commons.

86
00:05:12,421 --> 00:05:14,411
The Federation would have its own courts.

87
00:05:14,581 --> 00:05:16,191
Its own police.

88
00:05:16,381 --> 00:05:19,031
And most importantly:
its own armed forces.

89
00:05:19,922 --> 00:05:23,172
Not an army that countries lend
to it when they feel like it.

90
00:05:23,862 --> 00:05:26,912
An actual military belonging
to the Federation of Earth.

91
00:05:29,582 --> 00:05:32,212
Imagine that one country
invades its neighbour.

92
00:05:32,902 --> 00:05:35,772
The world government declares:
"This is aggression."

93
00:05:36,763 --> 00:05:39,813
And instead of: "We are considering
another round of sanctions..."

94
00:05:40,303 --> 00:05:44,093
the response is: "Withdraw your
forces within forty-eight hours."

95
00:05:44,923 --> 00:05:49,173
And if they don't? World forces
begin pushing the aggressor back.

96
00:05:49,963 --> 00:05:51,613
No months of negotiations.

97
00:05:52,143 --> 00:05:54,553
No waiting to see which
countries will join.

98
00:05:55,433 --> 00:05:57,553
No: "We couldn't reach an
agreement in the Security

99
00:05:57,633 --> 00:06:01,323
Council." There would simply
be a higher authority.

100
00:06:02,294 --> 00:06:04,674
Just as a state can tell
an individual: "You cannot

101
00:06:04,754 --> 00:06:08,424
do this." And if they
don't, the police intervene.

102
00:06:11,104 --> 00:06:13,854
Because a world government
would have enormous power.

103
00:06:14,624 --> 00:06:18,994
And that immediately raises another
question: What happens if it goes wrong?

104
00:06:19,714 --> 00:06:24,534
Today, one government can become
authoritarian. People can leave.

105
00:06:24,614 --> 00:06:28,904
They can overthrow it. Other countries
can provide a counterbalance.

106
00:06:29,574 --> 00:06:31,164
There can be democratic allies.

107
00:06:32,125 --> 00:06:34,215
But if there is only
one world government...

108
00:06:34,445 --> 00:06:36,535
where do you escape from it?

109
00:06:38,135 --> 00:06:39,565
Do we all move to Mars?

110
00:06:40,621 --> 00:06:42,581
So a world government
would need something that

111
00:06:42,661 --> 00:06:45,271
may be even more important
than its military.

112
00:06:46,181 --> 00:06:48,391
Extremely strong checks and balances.

113
00:06:51,261 --> 00:06:54,691
The ideal system might therefore
look more like a federation.

114
00:06:55,721 --> 00:06:58,771
The global level would have
only clearly defined powers.

115
00:06:59,621 --> 00:07:02,691
Countries would keep most of
their existing responsibilities.

116
00:07:03,798 --> 00:07:06,528
Brazil would still decide
how its schools work.

117
00:07:07,218 --> 00:07:10,088
France would still decide
how its roads are built.

118
00:07:10,778 --> 00:07:13,228
Japan would still run
its healthcare system.

119
00:07:13,946 --> 00:07:16,406
But if one country wanted
to start a war against its

120
00:07:16,486 --> 00:07:20,116
neighbour, that would no longer
be entirely its own business.

121
00:07:21,018 --> 00:07:24,848
Because a war between two countries
can threaten the entire planet.

122
00:07:24,958 --> 00:07:28,948
And that could be the boundary
between national and global authority.

123
00:07:32,038 --> 00:07:34,908
And this is, I think, the
most interesting part.

124
00:07:35,438 --> 00:07:38,198
Maybe a world government will
not appear because one day

125
00:07:38,278 --> 00:07:41,648
everyone decides: "A world
government would be nice."

126
00:07:42,504 --> 00:07:44,594
History suggests something different.

127
00:07:45,464 --> 00:07:48,744
Major political changes often
happen because a problem appears

128
00:07:48,824 --> 00:07:51,914
that is larger than the
political units dealing with it.

129
00:07:54,804 --> 00:07:59,604
Imagine that one day we detect
something strange. There is no doubt.

130
00:07:59,684 --> 00:08:01,554
It is an artificial signal.

131
00:08:01,824 --> 00:08:04,374
And it is coming from another star system.

132
00:08:05,187 --> 00:08:07,697
It isn't noise. It isn't
a natural phenomenon.

133
00:08:08,327 --> 00:08:09,737
Someone is out there.

134
00:08:10,469 --> 00:08:14,139
And they send us a message.
What do we do? Who answers?

135
00:08:15,132 --> 00:08:18,982
The President of the United
States? The President of China?

136
00:08:19,532 --> 00:08:24,672
The Secretary-General of the United
Nations? The European Union? India?

137
00:08:24,752 --> 00:08:25,962
Or everyone at once?

138
00:08:26,861 --> 00:08:29,431
They probably won't ask
who runs one country.

139
00:08:30,001 --> 00:08:32,731
They might ask: "Who
represents planet Earth?"

140
00:08:33,572 --> 00:08:36,982
And suddenly, we have a
problem. We don't have anyone.

141
00:08:39,862 --> 00:08:43,432
Maybe first contact wouldn't create
a world government overnight.

142
00:08:44,422 --> 00:08:46,972
But it could create enormous
pressure for coordination.

143
00:08:48,111 --> 00:08:50,511
We would need a common
diplomatic protocol.

144
00:08:50,591 --> 00:08:53,021
Joint scientific teams.

145
00:08:53,631 --> 00:08:55,341
A common defence strategy.

146
00:08:56,147 --> 00:08:58,607
And above all, someone
who could say: "This

147
00:08:58,687 --> 00:09:00,817
is the official position of humanity."

148
00:09:01,811 --> 00:09:04,411
Suddenly, the question: "Why
should we have some kind

149
00:09:04,491 --> 00:09:07,741
of world authority?" would
have a very practical answer.

150
00:09:08,511 --> 00:09:10,381
Because someone has to speak for Earth.

151
00:09:12,861 --> 00:09:16,171
We don't have to wait for an
extraterrestrial civilisation.

152
00:09:16,661 --> 00:09:19,851
The same pressure could come
from another global threat.

153
00:09:20,703 --> 00:09:23,033
An asteroid on a collision
course with Earth.

154
00:09:23,783 --> 00:09:25,233
A global pandemic.

155
00:09:25,823 --> 00:09:27,373
A technological catastrophe.

156
00:09:27,863 --> 00:09:30,733
Or something we cannot even predict today.

157
00:09:31,535 --> 00:09:34,695
The more interconnected the
world becomes, the more problems

158
00:09:34,775 --> 00:09:38,465
there will be that no individual
country can solve on its own.

159
00:09:39,141 --> 00:09:42,531
And maybe one day we will
realise that we have two choices.

160
00:09:43,621 --> 00:09:45,461
Either we have two
hundred countries trying

161
00:09:45,541 --> 00:09:48,191
to negotiate every time something happens.

162
00:09:49,031 --> 00:09:52,941
Or we create a higher level of government
for problems that affect everyone.

163
00:09:55,701 --> 00:09:59,031
Thousands of years ago, humans
mostly lived in small communities.

164
00:09:59,541 --> 00:10:02,591
Then came cities. Then kingdoms.

165
00:10:02,761 --> 00:10:04,511
Then nation-states.

166
00:10:04,801 --> 00:10:06,211
Then international organisations.

167
00:10:08,304 --> 00:10:11,594
And today, we are dealing with
problems that are genuinely planetary.

168
00:10:12,144 --> 00:10:15,454
Maybe the next step isn't:
"Get rid of countries."

169
00:10:16,423 --> 00:10:18,933
Maybe it is: "Add another
layer above them."

170
00:10:21,313 --> 00:10:24,423
Just as towns didn't disappear
when countries emerged.

171
00:10:24,553 --> 00:10:27,563
There would simply be
another level above them.

172
00:10:29,903 --> 00:10:31,453
That is far from certain.

173
00:10:32,383 --> 00:10:35,873
A world government could potentially
end wars between countries.

174
00:10:36,723 --> 00:10:39,713
It could be much more effective
at dealing with global problems.

175
00:10:40,627 --> 00:10:44,857
But it would also create the most powerful
political institution in human history.

176
00:10:45,737 --> 00:10:47,487
And if we designed it badly...

177
00:10:48,197 --> 00:10:51,367
we could create something much
worse than the world we have today.

178
00:10:52,220 --> 00:10:57,410
So perhaps the real question isn't:
"Do we need a world government?" It is:

179
00:10:58,206 --> 00:11:01,066
"Can we ever build a world
government that is powerful enough to

180
00:11:01,146 --> 00:11:05,456
stop countries, but weak enough
that it cannot enslave humanity?"

181
00:11:06,174 --> 00:11:07,924
And that is a hell of a problem.

182
00:11:10,934 --> 00:11:13,164
Today we live in a strange situation.

183
00:11:14,274 --> 00:11:17,144
Inside countries, we have
laws, courts, and police.

184
00:11:18,034 --> 00:11:21,064
But between countries, there is
still something resembling anarchy.

185
00:11:22,085 --> 00:11:24,855
Countries can negotiate.
They can sign treaties.

186
00:11:25,685 --> 00:11:29,055
They can create organisations.
They can impose sanctions.

187
00:11:29,991 --> 00:11:34,701
But there is still nobody above them with
the ultimate authority to say: "Enough."

188
00:11:35,612 --> 00:11:38,242
Maybe it will stay that
way for hundreds of years.

189
00:11:38,332 --> 00:11:42,722
Or maybe something will happen that
changes it within a single decade.

190
00:11:43,603 --> 00:11:48,963
Maybe a catastrophe. Maybe a technological
breakthrough. Maybe a common threat.

191
00:11:49,043 --> 00:11:50,243
Or maybe...

192
00:11:50,935 --> 00:11:53,105
one day, someone will answer our message.

193
00:11:55,845 --> 00:11:58,715
And humanity will realise that
to the rest of the universe,

194
00:11:59,205 --> 00:12:02,585
we are not the United States,
or China, or Europe, or

195
00:12:02,665 --> 00:12:05,835
any of the countries we spend
our lives arguing about.

196
00:12:06,587 --> 00:12:07,977
We are simply... Earth.

197
00:12:10,727 --> 00:12:12,667
And for the first time,
we may have to answer

198
00:12:12,747 --> 00:12:15,277
the question: "Who speaks for humanity?"
